WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Meet SocialAgent. This is a real walkthrough

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.380
of the application, using a fictional neighborhood

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.320
café. We will turn a rising conversation

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.200
into three channel drafts, review real media,

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.920
simulate delivery, and answer a customer. Nothing

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.000
in this demonstration publishes to a real

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:17.720
social account.

00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:20.533
First, the agent looks for a worthwhile

00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.833
conversation. Iced coffee is gaining interest in

00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:26.413
our demo data. The evidence panel explains

00:00:26.413 --> 00:00:29.133
the recent count, the baseline, and the

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:32.913
sources. Stale or irrelevant topics can be

00:00:32.913 --> 00:00:35.213
skipped. This is a reason to create

00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:37.793
something useful, not a quota to fill.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.640
We choose Create drafts. A background worker

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.120
uses the café’s approved facts and brand

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.420
voice, then creates separate Instagram, YouTube, and

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.160
LinkedIn versions. It also renders an actual

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.720
image and video. A draft remains in

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.560
review until a person approves it.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.560
Here is the Instagram draft, with a

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.220
real branded design and editable copy. The

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.720
copy draws on approved brand knowledge rather

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.320
than an invented offer. The reviewer can

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.400
inspect the references and save a new

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.440
revision. Editing the content invalidates its previous

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.520
approval, so an old approval cannot publish

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.100
a new draft.

00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:19.433
The YouTube version includes a playable vertical

00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:22.933
video. This is a real MP4, rendered

00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:25.593
on the CPU, with text and scene

00:01:25.593 --> 00:01:29.033
timing. No graphics card is required. Each

00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:31.613
destination has its own copy and assets,

00:01:31.793 --> 00:01:33.833
and the video is available to download.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.960
Now we review the LinkedIn version and

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.020
approve tomorrow’s schedule for each channel. Approval

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.780
binds the account, content revision, assets, visibility,

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.420
and time. Each destination is handled independently.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.560
YouTube visibility remains explicit; a private upload

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.940
is never presented as public publication.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.900
Advancing the demo clock lets the worker

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.800
process the approved schedules. The calendar now

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.120
shows a separate simulated receipt for every

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.700
channel. In live operation, provider checkpoints help

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.380
recover uploads. An uncertain publication outcome stops

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.840
for inspection instead of blindly reposting.

00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:15.553
Finally, a customer asks whether the café

00:02:15.553 --> 00:02:18.793
is open tomorrow. The agent prepares a

00:02:18.793 --> 00:02:21.753
dated reply using the brand’s local timezone

00:02:21.753 --> 00:02:25.093
and approved hours. The person reviewing it

00:02:25.093 --> 00:02:28.613
still presses Approve before it sends. New

00:02:28.613 --> 00:02:32.533
messages invalidate stale replies. The result is

00:02:32.533 --> 00:02:35.593
less routine work, with people keeping control.
